<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Homeridae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Homeridae, &quot;sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>&quot;, were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> or clan of rhapsodes on the Greek island of Chios who claimed descent from Homer and recited his poems. Mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and later writers, they supported Chios's claim to be Homer's birthplace, and the Homeric Hymn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> speaks of a blind poet who lived on rocky Chios. The name was later used more widely for performers and scholars of Homer.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In classical Greek the name should mean children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, on the pattern of the Asclepiadae, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of physicians called children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a>. Yet the existence of the Homeridae is attested while that of Homer is not, and the common noun homeros means hostage, so it was suggested even in antiquity that they were really descendants of hostages. One story told that the women of Chios once went mad at a festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and fought the men, and that peace came only when brides and bridegrooms were exchanged as pledges, whose descendants were the Homeridae. Some have taken the further step of treating Homer as a back-formation from the guild's name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The evidence belongs to the late sixth, fifth and fourth centuries BCE, after which nothing more is heard of them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, in a poem of about 485 BCE, says that the Homeridae, singers of stitched words, usually begin with an address to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. Isocrates, writing around 350 BCE, reports their story that Helen appeared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> in a dream and told him to compose a poem about the Trojan expedition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> mentions them reciting hymns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eros">Eros</a>, and in his Ion the rhapsode Ion claims he should be crowned by the Homeridae.</p><p>Later antiquarian sources, of uncertain value, add details. A commentary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> names Cynaethus of Chios as a member of a group that composed new poems and attached them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s works, and calls him the author of the Homeric Hymn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and the first to perform Homer at Syracuse. The local historians Acusilaus and Hellanicus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> derived the name from Homer, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a> denied it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Homeridae appear to have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of oral performers who claimed to inherit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s tradition, travelled widely like other rhapsodes and were perhaps based on Chios. They recited poems ascribed to Homer, doubtless including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, and told stories about how the poems came to be. Their influence on the early transmission of the texts, though impossible to measure, was surely conservative. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Pisistratus</a>, tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, made a fixed text compulsory, they switched to a version they had approved themselves, helping to produce some of the first authoritative texts of Homer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Homeridae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herodotus credits Glaucus of Chios with inventing the welding of iron, a family of sculptors descended from Archermus worked there in the sixth century BCE, and the <b>Homeridae</b>, a guild of rhapsodes who claimed descent from Homer, kept the island's claim to be his birthplace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
